From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bharat Dintakurti <bdintakurti@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 17:10:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NVIDIA: SAUCE: ACPI: processor_idle: Link LPI domains to CPU
 consumers

BugLink: https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/2167301

The generated leaf and parent domains do not influence genpd policy until
they form a topology with a CPU-associated consumer.

Link each processor leaf below its parent containers while walking the
extracted _LPI levels. Record every new link and domain in one construction
transaction. On failure, remove links from the leaf toward the root before
destroying newly created domains from the root toward the leaf, without
disturbing shared domains created for another CPU.

A genpd CPU mask is propagated through the ancestry when a consumer
attaches. Reject a missing parent link once the child subtree already owns
CPUs, so every CPU-visible hierarchy is completed before consumers are
published.

Register a private bus for per-CPU virtual consumers and attach each
consumer to its leaf domain using the physical CPU as the genpd affinity
source. Establish and release the initial runtime-PM reference together
with device attachment, and extend domain removal to preserve parent links
and child references on failure.

On PREEMPT_RT, pair every direct genpd resume with a prior direct suspend.
Track that state separately from the logical online reference and balance
it before detaching the consumer, avoiding an underflow of genpd's
system-suspend counter during initial setup.

This commit changes topology ownership only. The cpuidle tables still
contain direct callbacks and PSCI OSI remains disabled.

